==Trivia==
*Tackey's name may be a combination of ''tack'' (a small, sharp nail, likely referencing the sharp spikes on Tackey's projectiles), ''tacky'' (kitsch), 滝 ''taki'' (waterfall) and 竜の落とし子 ''tatsu no otoshigo'' (seahorse).
*Tackey's Japanese name, Tatchan, is a reference to the baseball manga ''[[wikipedia:Touch (manga)|Touch]]'' (タッチ ''Tatchi'' in Japanese).
**"Tatchan" or "Tat-chan" can also be an affectionate nickname for Tatsuya Uesugi, the main protagonist of the story.
**The first line of Tackey's Japanese description is an exact mirror of Tatsuya's role in ''Touch'': after his younger brother Kazuya dies in a traffic accident, he takes over for him and fulfills his childhood friend Minami's dream in Kazuya's stead.
**Tatsuya being referenced by a seahorse enemy may be because one of the Japanese terms for seahorse is '''''tatsu''''' ''no otoshigo''.
*Tackey and its variants all reference baseball manga or players with their Japanese names and descriptions. Tackey and [[Ackey]] reference ''Touch'', [[Hackey]] references ''[[wikipedia:Star of the Giants|Star of the Giants]]'', and [[Ange Lackey]] references [[wikipedia:Babe Ruth|Babe Ruth]]. Ange Lackey's English name may also reference [[wikipedia:John Lackey|John Lackey]].
*The deceased younger brother mentioned in Tackey's Japanese description may be Ange Lackey, whose Japanese description states that they died from overwork.
*Tackey may specifically be based on the [[wikipedia:Estuary seahorse|estuary seahorse]], whose Japanese name, 黒海馬 ''kuroumiuma'', literally translates to "black seahorse".
*The name of Tackey's debut stage, [[Thorny Dialogue]], references its attack animation, in which it fires a thorny projectile from its mouth.
*Tackey's English name is identical to [[Gobble]]'s Japanese name.
*Due to [[The Battle Cats Unite!]] importing Tackey when enemies still had their trait listed after their name, in [[The Battle Cats Unite!/Patch 1.0.9 Update|Patch 1.0.9]], Tackey was renamed from ''Tackey (Black)'' to ''Tackey (Dark)'', making it the only known case of a unit having its renamed trait using the old trait listing scheme.

